        "id": 1121354
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-11-02T19:42:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Per IM discussion with nnk@, there are already selinux rules to check bluetooth socket creation. Currently only one security domain (\"hal_bluetooth_btlinux\") is allowed to create bluetooth sockets and that is more strict then the euid check in kernel. So we can remove this part as well. No userspace change required.",
      "parentUuid": "d07906f1_2f309835",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 116,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 119,
        "endChar": 6
      },
      "revId": "6fcfd1baa19aacdbb430f8e4d4d263bdf46be3c9",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7beaf65b_87ab887f",
        "filename": "net/bluetooth/af_bluetooth.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 119,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003966
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-11-02T20:01:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Prior to kernel 4.11, when a network address family (such as bluetooth) was not specifically known to the SELinux subsystem, the permission checks were performed against the generic \"socket\" class. \n\nAfter https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/da69a5306ab92e07224da54aafee8b1dccf024f6 , SELinux gained specific knowledge of bluetooth sockets, allowing policy to be written specifically against bluetooth sockets as opposed to the generic \"socket\" socket.\n\nNo userspace change is required. Android enabled extended socket permissions in https://android-review.googlesource.com/c/platform/system/sepolicy/+/333267 .",
      "parentUuid": "65debd76_a9a4c255",
      "range": {
